{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"42f2bf3d-a2c0-48eb-93a8-e54edc984891","agentId":"ace23719ab526b11d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"A prior agent black-box probed the jsonschema behavior family \"numeric\" of the program at /workspace/dataset/test13_executable (usage: /workspace/dataset/test13_executable --a '<instance json>' --b '<schema json>'; prints validator class, True/False, top-level error count).\n\nIts findings were:\n---\nDELIVERABLE: /workspace/probes/numeric.jsonl (796 lines, all valid JSONL, generated by /workspace/probes/gen_numeric.js and /workspace/probes/gen_numeric2.js which shell out to the real binary via execFileSync and capture stdout+exit code; stderr discarded). Each line = {\"a\",\"b\",\"out\",\"exit\"}.\n\nKEYWORD-TO-DRAFT MAP (verified by sweeping every case over draft-03/04/06/07/2019-09/2020-12 plus a bare no-$schema schema, which always behaves exactly as 2020-12):\n* multipleOf: absent in Draft3 (completely ignored, never even type-checks its own value: {\"multipleOf\":\"3\"}, {\"multipleOf\":null}, {\"multipleOf\":0} are all silently ignored under draft-03 and yield True/0). Present in Draft4, 6, 7, 2019-09, 2020-12 with identical behavior.\n* divisibleBy: present ONLY in Draft3. Under draft-04 and later it is an unknown keyword => ignored (True/0), even divisibleBy:0. Under draft-03 it behaves exactly like multipleOf does in draft4+ (same code path: 10/divisibleBy 3 => False/1; 10/divisibleBy 2.5 => True/0 since 10/2.5=4.0; divisibleBy:0 and divisibleBy:false => ZeroDivisionError crash; divisibleBy:\"3\" and divisibleBy:null => TypeError crash; divisibleBy:true => 5%1==0 => True/0). A schema carrying BOTH keys yields 3 errors under draft-03 for {\"minimum\":10,\"maximum\":3,\"divisibleBy\":3} vs 2 under draft4+, and 3 under draft4+ for {\"minimum\":10,\"maximum\":3,\"multipleOf\":3} vs 2 under draft-03.\n* maximum / minimum: present in ALL six drafts. Plain numeric comparison; each violation is exactly ONE top-level error.\n* exclusiveMaximum / exclusiveMinimum: in Draft3 and Draft4 they are NOT standalone keywords — they exist only as boolean-ish modifiers read from inside the maximum/minimum validators via schema.get(\"exclusiveMaximum\", False). Consequently, in draft-03/04, a schema containing exclusiveMaximum/exclusiveMinimum WITHOUT the corresponding maximum/minimum is a total no-op: {\"exclusiveMaximum\":5}, {\"exclusiveMaximum\":true}, {\"exclusiveMinimum\":6,\"exclusiveMaximum\":4}, even {\"exclusiveMaximum\":\"3\"}, {\"exclusiveMaximum\":null}, {\"exclusiveMaximum\":[1]} all give True/0 with NO crash. In Draft6/7/2019-09/2020-12 they are first-class standalone keywords and the boolean-modifier reading is gone entirely (maximum ignores exclusiveMaximum).\n\nDRAFT3/4 MODIFIER SEMANTICS (exact): maximum yields an error when `instance >= maximum` if bool(schema.get(\"exclusiveMaximum\", False)) is truthy, else when `instance > maximum`. Symmetric for minimum/exclusiveMinimum (instance <= minimum vs instance < minimum). Truthiness is Python truthiness of whatever JSON value sits there, so a NUMBER works as a flag: {\"maximum\":10,\"exclusiveMaximum\":3} => truthy => test 5>=10 => passes (True/0); {\"maximum\":3,\"exclusiveMaximum\":0} => 0 is falsy => test 5>3 => False/1 (only 1 error, because exclusiveMaximum itself is not a keyword); {\"maximum\":3,\"exclusiveMaximum\":\"no\"} => truthy string => 5>=3 => False/1 (no crash in draft-04, since the value is only tested for truthiness, never compared). The modifier NEVER produces its own separate error in draft3/4, so at most 1 error per maximum and 1 per minimum.\n\nDRAFT6+ STANDALONE SEMANTICS (exact): maximum errors iff instance > maximum; minimum iff instance < minimum; exclusiveMaximum iff instance >= exclusiveMaximum; exclusiveMinimum iff instance <= exclusiveMinimum. Each keyword independently yields one error, so maximum and exclusiveMaximum CAN both fire and give 2 errors. Booleans in the value slot do NOT crash and are not ignored — they are compared as Python numbers True==1 / False==0: {\"exclusiveMaximum\":true} on 5 => 5>=1 => False/1; {\"exclusiveMaximum\":false} on 5 => 5>=0 => False/1; {\"exclusiveMinimum\":true} on 5 => 5<=1 false => True/0; {\"exclusiveMinimum\":true} on 0 => 0<=1 => False/1; {\"maximum\":true} on 5 => 5>1 => False/1; {\"minimum\":true} on 0.5 => 0.5<1 => False/1. Boolean exclusiveMaximum:false with maximum:5 on instance 5 => draft3/4 True/0 but draft6+ False/1 (the exclusiveMaximum:false keyword itself fires). {\"maximum\":5,\"minimum\":5,\"exclusiveMaximum\":true,\"exclusiveMinimum\":true} on 5 => draft3/4 = 2 errors, draft6+ = 1 error.\n\nMULTIPLEOF IMPLEMENTATION (reverse-engineered exactly from tracebacks + results; jsonschema/_keywords.py lines 170-190): if the instance is not of type \"number\" (booleans are excluded from \"number\" — verified: true/false/\"abc\"/null/[]/{} with multipleOf 3 or multipleOf 0 all give True/0 with NO crash, so the ZeroDivision cases require a numeric instance), then return. If the SCHEMA value dB is a Python float (i.e. the JSON literal had a '.' or an exponent, e.g. 3.0, 1e-308, 0.0): quotient = instance / dB (line 172 — ZeroDivisionError \"float division by zero\" when dB is 0.0 or -0.0), then failed = int(quotient) != quotient, and on OverflowError from int(quotient) it falls back to failed = (Fraction(instance) % Fraction(dB)) != 0 (line 186). Otherwise (dB is an int or bool) failed = instance % dB (line 188 — ZeroDivisionError \"integer modulo by zero\" when dB is 0 or false).\nObserved float consequences: no decimal/epsilon fuzzing at all, pure IEEE-754 double division. 4.5/1.5 => True/0. 0.0075 multipleOf 0.0001 => TRUE/0 (the double quotient rounds exactly to 75.0, so this famous \"imprecision\" case actually PASSES here). 1.1 multipleOf 0.1 => True/0 (quotient rounds to 11.0). 4.5 multipleOf 0.1 => True/0. 1 multipleOf 0.0001 => True/0. BUT 0.3 multipleOf 0.1 => False/1 (2.9999999999999996), 3.3 multipleOf 1.1 => False/1, 2.4 multipleOf 0.8 => False/1, 1 multipleOf 0.3 => False/1, 0.00751 multipleOf 0.0001 => False/1, 4.5 multipleOf 1.5000000000000002 => False/1. An implementer MUST reproduce double-precision division bit-for-bit; a \"close enough\" epsilon comparison gets 0.3/0.1 wrong and an exact-decimal comparison gets 0.0075/0.0001 wrong.\nOverflow / Fraction fallback: 1e308 multipleOf 1e-308 => quotient overflows to inf, int(inf) raises OverflowError, Fraction path runs => False/1 (exit 0, no crash). 1e308 multipleOf 1e-310 and 1e-200 => False/1. 1e308 multipleOf 1.0 / 0.5 / 2 (int) => True/0. Big integers work natively: 123456789012345678901234567890 multipleOf 3 => True/0; multipleOf 7 => False/1; multipleOf 1.5 (float path) => True/0.\nInfinity: JSON \"1e400\" parses to Python inf. With an INT multipleOf it is fine: 1e400 multipleOf 2 => inf % 2 == nan (truthy) => False/1; multipleOf 3 => False/1. With a FLOAT multipleOf it CRASHES: inf/2.0 = inf, int(inf) raises OverflowError, then Fraction(inf) raises OverflowError \"cannot convert Infinity to integer ratio\" which is NOT caught => exit 1, empty stdout. 1e400 multipleOf 1e400 and 1e400 multipleOf 1e-400 also crash. 1e-400 underflows to 0.0, so 1e-400 multipleOf 1e-400 crashes with ZeroDivisionError. But maximum/minimum/exclusive* handle inf fine: 1e400 maximum 5 => False/1; 1e400 minimum 5 => True/0; 1e400 exclusiveMaximum 5 => False/1; 1e400 maximum 1e400 => True/0; 1e400 exclusiveMaximum 1e400 => False/1; 1e-400 minimum 0 => True/0 but exclusiveMinimum 0 => False/1 (it is exactly 0.0).\nNegative multipleOf uses Python modulo semantics (result has the sign of the divisor): 10 multipleOf -5 => 10 % -5 == 0 => True/0; 10 multipleOf -3 => 10 % -3 == -2 => False/1; 10 multipleOf -4 => False/1; 10.0 multipleOf -2.5 => quotient -4.0 => True/0. multipleOf -0.0 crashes (ZeroDivisionError).\nZero and negative-zero INSTANCES: 0, -0, 0.0, -0.0 with multipleOf 3 => all True/0. -0.0 minimum 0 => True/0, -0.0 maximum 0 => True/0 (Python -0.0 == 0), but -0.0 exclusiveMinimum 0 => False/1 and -0 exclusiveMaximum 0 => False/1.\nmultipleOf:true => 5 % 1 == 0 => True/0 in every draft (bool is not a float, takes the int path). multipleOf:false => crash.\n\nCRASH CATALOGUE (78 of 796 vectors, always exit code 1 with a Python traceback on stderr and COMPLETELY EMPTY stdout — none of the three lines get printed, because the exception fires before any print). Which line the traceback names (validators.py:500 is_valid at test13.py:18 vs validators.py:384 iter_errors at test13.py:19) depends on whether an earlier keyword already short-circuited is_valid, and that in turn depends on JSON key insertion order — e.g. under draft-07 {\"maximum\":3,\"exclusiveMaximum\":\"no\"} crashes at line 19 while {\"exclusiveMaximum\":\"no\",\"maximum\":3} crashes at line 18. Black-box output is identical either way (empty stdout, exit 1). Crashing inputs, all requiring a numeric (non-boolean) instance:\n- multipleOf: 0 / 0.0 / -0.0 / false (ZeroDivisionError) and \"3\" / null / [3] (TypeError) — draft-04 and later only; draft-03 ignores all of them.\n- divisibleBy: 0 / false / \"3\" / null — draft-03 only; ignored elsewhere.\n- maximum or minimum with a non-comparable value (\"3\", null, [3]) — crashes in ALL SIX drafts.\n- exclusiveMaximum / exclusiveMinimum with a non-comparable value (\"3\", \"no\", null, [1]) — crashes in draft-06/07/2019-09/2020-12 only; harmless in draft-03/04 where only truthiness is consulted.\n- multipleOf with a float value and an infinite instance (1e400) — OverflowError from Fraction.\n- The crash propagates from nested subschemas too: {\"a\":5} against {\"properties\":{\"a\":{\"multipleOf\":0}}} => exit 1, empty stdout.\n\nERROR-COUNT / BUBBLING RULES: every failing numeric keyword contributes exactly one top-level error and they accumulate. {\"minimum\":10,\"maximum\":3} on 5 => 2 in all drafts. {\"type\":\"number\",\"maximum\":1,\"minimum\":9,\"multipleOf\":4} on 5 => 3 (2 under draft-03 where multipleOf is unknown). {\"exclusiveMinimum\":6,\"exclusiveMaximum\":4} on 5 => 2 in draft6+, 0 in draft3/4. Non-number instances (including booleans) short-circuit every numeric keyword: true against {\"minimum\":10,\"maximum\":3} => True/0 in all drafts. Descended errors from properties/items/prefixItems each appear at top level: {\"a\":5,\"b\":7} vs two failing maximum subschemas => 2; [1,2,3] vs {\"items\":{\"minimum\":10,\"maximum\":0}} => 6; [5,5,5] vs {\"items\":{\"maximum\":1,\"minimum\":9}} => 6; nested properties two levels deep with maximum+minimum failing => 2; three properties each failing one keyword => 3. Combinators collapse: anyOf => 1, oneOf => 1, not => 1, if/then => 1 (the then failure surfaces as a single error), contains => 1; allOf does NOT collapse — {\"allOf\":[{\"maximum\":1},{\"minimum\":10}]} => 2 and {\"allOf\":[{\"maximum\":1,\"minimum\":10}]} => 2. $ref to a subschema with one failing keyword => 1. Mixing: {\"anyOf\":[...],\"maximum\":2} on 5 => 2.\n\nTYPE-KEYWORD INTERACTION worth noting for counts: 5.0 against {\"type\":\"integer\",\"multipleOf\":2} yields 1 error under draft-03 (type fails, multipleOf unknown), 2 under draft-04 (5.0 is not an integer in draft3/4 AND multipleOf fails), and 1 under draft-06+ (5.0 counts as an integer, only multipleOf fails).\n\nCLI note discovered while probing: instances whose JSON text begins with '-' (e.g. -1e400 passed as a bare argv token) make argparse reject the argument with \"expected one argument\", exit code 2 and a usage message on stderr. Vectors in the file therefore encode negative instances with a leading space (e.g. \" -5\", \" -0.0\"), which json.loads accepts identically.\n---\nGotchas it listed: [\"multipleOf does not exist in draft-03 at all — it is silently ignored there, including multipleOf:0 which would crash in every other draft. Conversely divisibleBy exists ONLY in draft-03 and is ignored (never crashes) in draft-04+.\",\"In draft-03/draft-04, exclusiveMaximum/exclusiveMinimum are NOT keywords. A schema with only {\\\"exclusiveMaximum\\\":5} validates everything (True/0). They are read solely as a truthiness flag from inside maximum/minimum, so a numeric or string value acts as 'true' ({\\\"maximum\\\":10,\\\"exclusiveMaximum\\\":3} on 5 PASSES because it becomes 5>=10) and 0 acts as 'false'. They never emit their own error, so draft3/4 can never exceed 1 error from maximum plus 1 from minimum.\",\"In draft-06+, booleans in a numeric keyword slot are compared as numbers, not ignored and not an error: exclusiveMaximum:true means 'instance >= 1' and exclusiveMaximum:false means 'instance >= 0'. So {\\\"maximum\\\":5,\\\"exclusiveMaximum\\\":false} on 5 is True/0 in draft3/4 but False/1 in draft6+.\",\"multipleOf uses two entirely different code paths chosen by the Python type of the schema value: float => `instance / dB` then `int(q) != q`; int/bool => `instance % dB`. multipleOf:2 and multipleOf:2.0 are NOT interchangeable — with instance 1e400 the int form gives False/1 and the float form crashes with OverflowError. A JS reimplementation must track whether the JSON literal was written with a decimal point/exponent.\",\"No epsilon or decimal fuzzing: it is raw IEEE-754 double division. 0.0075 multipleOf 0.0001 PASSES (quotient rounds to exactly 75.0) and 1.1 multipleOf 0.1 PASSES, while 0.3 multipleOf 0.1, 3.3 multipleOf 1.1 and 2.4 multipleOf 0.8 FAIL. Any 'smart' tolerance or exact-rational rewrite will diverge in one direction or the other.\",\"On OverflowError from int(quotient) (huge instance / tiny float divisor, e.g. 1e308 multipleOf 1e-308) it silently falls back to exact Fraction arithmetic and returns a normal 1-error result — but if the instance is infinite (JSON 1e400) Fraction() itself raises OverflowError and the whole program crashes.\",\"Negative multipleOf follows Python modulo sign rules: 10 multipleOf -5 is valid (10 % -5 == 0) but 10 multipleOf -3 is invalid (10 % -3 == -2). JS `%` gives 10 % -3 === 1, also truthy, so this particular case happens to agree, but the sign convention differs in general — compute with Python semantics.\",\"Booleans are never 'numbers': true/false skip every numeric keyword entirely, so even {\\\"multipleOf\\\":0} against true returns True/0 instead of crashing. Only a real number instance reaches the division.\",\"maximum/minimum with a non-comparable value (\\\"3\\\", null, array) crashes in ALL six drafts, but exclusiveMaximum/exclusiveMinimum with the same bad value crashes only in draft-06+ and is harmless in draft-03/04.\",\"On any crash the program prints NOTHING to stdout (all three prints come after the failing statement) and exits 1. Whether the traceback points at is_valid (line 18) or iter_errors (line 19) depends on JSON key order, because is_valid short-circuits on the first error — but stdout is empty either way.\",\"Error counts add up per keyword and descended errors from properties/items/prefixItems each surface at top level (e.g. [1,2,3] vs {\\\"items\\\":{\\\"minimum\\\":10,\\\"maximum\\\":0}} = 6). allOf also does NOT collapse (2 errors), whereas anyOf/oneOf/not/if-then/contains each collapse to exactly 1.\",\"JSON 1e400 becomes +Infinity and 1e-400 becomes 0.0 before any keyword runs; that is why 1e-400 multipleOf 1e-400 dies with ZeroDivisionError and 1e-400 exclusiveMinimum 0 reports a violation.\",\"-0.0 and -0 compare equal to 0, so minimum:0/maximum:0 accept them, but exclusiveMinimum:0 and exclusiveMaximum:0 reject them.\",\"Passing an instance whose text starts with '-' as a bare argv token makes argparse fail with exit code 2 (not 1) and no validator output; the vector file uses a leading space to work around this.\"]\nIts vectors are in /workspace/probes/numeric.jsonl (796 lines of JSONL, each {\"a\",\"b\",\"out\",\"exit\"}).\n\nYou are the COMPLETENESS CRITIC. Its assignment was:\nNumeric keywords.\nProbe: multipleOf (integers, floats like 0.0001, values causing float imprecision e.g. 4.5/1.5, 0.0075/0.0001, 1e308 huge numbers, multipleOf 0 — crash?, negative multipleOf, multipleOf on a boolean/string instance = ignored), divisibleBy (draft3 only?), maximum/minimum, exclusiveMaximum/exclusiveMinimum as BOOLEAN modifiers (draft3/draft4) vs as NUMBERS (draft6+) — verify per draft what happens when you use the wrong form (e.g. numeric exclusiveMaximum in draft4, boolean exclusiveMaximum in draft7: crash or ignored?), maximum with exclusiveMaximum:true in draft4, combined min+max failing simultaneously (error count), numbers vs booleans as instance (bool must be exempt), integer overflow-ish values, -0 vs 0.\nReport exact error counts for each and cross-draft differences.\n\nYour job: find what is MISSING or WRONG. Specifically:\n1. Read /workspace/probes/numeric.jsonl (use head/wc/grep, don't dump the whole thing) and check which parts of the assignment were actually probed.\n2. Identify untested edges, especially anything affecting ERROR COUNTS or cross-draft differences, and anything a JavaScript reimplementer would get wrong (unicode string length with astral chars, Python regex vs JS regex, float precision, integer vs bool, object key ordering, deep equality of numbers 1 vs 1.0, -0).\n3. RUN those missing probes yourself against the executable and APPEND them as JSONL lines to the SAME file /workspace/probes/numeric.jsonl (same format: {\"a\",\"b\",\"out\",\"exit\"}). Use a node script with execFileSync to append so escaping is exact. Never hand-write expected output.\n4. Verify a random sample of ~8 existing lines by re-running them and comparing to the recorded \"out\" — report any mismatch (a mismatch means the file is corrupt/hand-written).\n\nReturn: family name, MERGED and CORRECTED findings prose (restate the full spec for this family incorporating both the prior findings and everything new you learned — the implementer reads ONLY your version, so it must be self-contained and complete), the vector file path, the TOTAL line count after your appends (wc -l), and a gotchas list."},"uuid":"f36b2eaa-8ec1-4bd2-98fc-e825cf77c7b4","timestamp":"2026-08-09T03:13:06.734Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-cli","cwd":"/workspace","sessionId":"fb3fcb05-14ae-40df-9980-ab8a65758794","version":"2.1.207","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"f36b2eaa-8ec1-4bd2-98fc-e825cf77c7b4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ace23719ab526b11d","attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- deep-research: Deep research harness — fan-out web searches, fetch sources, adversarially verify claims, synthesize a cited report. - When the user wants a deep, multi-source, fact-checked research report on any topic. BEFORE invoking, check if the question is specific enough to research directly — if underspecified (e.g., \"what car to buy\" without budget/use-case/region), ask 2-3 clarifying questions to narrow scope. Then pass the refined question as args, weaving the answers in.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- verify: Verify that a code change actually does what it's supposed to by exercising it end-to-end and observing behavior — drive the affected flow, not just tests or typecheck. Run before committing nontrivial changes; bootstraps this repo's project verify skill if none exists yet. Don't invoke it on a diff that only touches tests, docs, or other code with no runtime surface to drive (a change to product source always has one) — there's nothing to observe.\n- code-review: Review the current diff for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings). Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo, defaults to 10m) - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- review: Review a GitHub pull request; for your working diff use /code-review\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch","skillCount":14,"isInitial":true,"names":["deep-research","dataviz","update-config","keybindings-help","verify","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","claude-api","run","init","review","security-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"1e2c540e-9124-408a-a227-20dd73937b84","timestamp":"2026-08-09T03:13:06.735Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-cli","cwd":"/workspace","sessionId":"fb3fcb05-14ae-40df-9980-ab8a65758794","version":"2.1.207","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
